  ],
  "prompt": "For sufficiently large input size n, which of these running times grows fastest?\n\n**A.** O(2ⁿ)\n**B.** O(n¹⁰⁰)\n**C.** O(n²⁰ · log n)\n**D.** O(n! / 2ⁿ)",
  "instructions": [
    "Choose the single best answer."
  ],
  "constraints": [],
  "options": [
    "O(2ⁿ)",
    "O(n¹⁰⁰)",
    "O(n²⁰ · log n)",
    "O(n! / 2ⁿ)"
  ],
  "correctOptionIndex": 3,
  "examples": [],
  "hints": [
    "Two options are exponential-flavored — compare those, not the polynomials.",
    "Stirling: n! ≈ (n/e)ⁿ · √(2πn), so n!/2ⁿ ≈ (n/(2e))ⁿ · √(2πn)."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "summary": "The correct answer is D — O(n! / 2ⁿ)",
    "answer": "O(n! / 2ⁿ)",
    "reasoning": [
      "Both O(n!/2ⁿ) and O(2ⁿ) are exponential-flavored; the polynomials never compete.",
      "Stirling: n! ≈ (n/e)ⁿ · √(2πn), so n!/2ⁿ ≈ (n/(2e))ⁿ · √(2πn), whose base (n/(2e)) itself grows with n.",
      "Therefore n!/2ⁿ grows faster than any fixed-base exponential — it is the fastest of the four."
    ],
    "whyNot": [
      "O(2ⁿ) — O(2ⁿ) is exponential and dwarfs every polynomial — but it is still dominated by the factorial-based option.",
      "O(n¹⁰⁰) — n¹⁰⁰ is a fixed polynomial; any exponential 2ᵏⁿ with k > 0 eventually overtakes it, no matter how large the exponent.",
      "O(n²⁰ · log n) — n²⁰ · log n is polynomial (times a logarithm), so both exponentials dominate it."
    ]
  },
